<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Federated Algorithm.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Federated Algorithm. ‣ 4.2 Memorization Factors ‣ 4 Results ‣ Exploring Cross-Client Memorization of Training Data in Large Language Models for Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> indicates that FedProx leads to higher memorization rates (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\sf MR_{Inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝖱</mi><mi>𝖨𝗇𝗍𝖾𝗋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\sf MR_{Inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\sf MR_{Intra}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝖱</mi><mi>𝖨𝗇𝗍𝗋𝖺</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\sf MR_{Intra}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Notably, it exhibits memorization in the classification task, which is absent in FedAvg.
This indicates that memorization varies across algorithms and should be evaluated independently.</p>
</div>
