</figure>
<div id="A7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\sf MR_{Intra}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝖱</mi><mi>𝖨𝗇𝗍𝗋𝖺</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\sf MR_{Intra}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\sf MR_{Inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝖱</mi><mi>𝖨𝗇𝗍𝖾𝗋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\sf MR_{Inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> tend to increase when top-p or top-k is used in most cases for both Qwen2.5-3B and Llama3.2-3B as shown in Table <a href="#A7.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ G.3 Memorization with Various Decoding Methods ‣ Appendix G Detailed Results ‣ Exploring Cross-Client Memorization of Training Data in Large Language Models for Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, suggesting better decoding methods lead to more memorization.</p>
</div>
</section>
